WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.360
Do you think that you would have a civil war?

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:03.400
Are you worried about that?

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.170
What do you think will happen if we do nothing?

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:06.600
Do you think more kids will be killed?

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:07.680
I'm not, I'm not arguing on behalf-

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.660
Do you think more concerts will be shot up,
more churches will--

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:10.800
I'm not arguing on behalf-

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.020
The court keeps allowing states to ban assault-style
weapons.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:19.480
Your inability to do even the common sense
stuff really frustrates me.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:22.740
So when do we say enough is enough?

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.800
A string of high-profile mass shootings over
the past few years

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.600
has spawned a movement that’s calling for
new legislation

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.359
to outlaw so-called assault-style weapons,

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.980
in particular the popular AR-15.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:43.360
Assault weapon bans are depicted as life-saving,
“common sense” policy.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:47.640
The problem is that the definition of an assault
weapon is totally arbitrary.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.140
An assault weapon is whatever is covered by
an assault weapon ban.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:54.060
Jacob Sullum is a senior editor at Reason
who writes about gun policy.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:57.800
The criteria that are used to identify assault
weapons

00:00:57.809 --> 00:01:02.200
are things that have little or nothing to
do with how useful or how deadly

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.740
an assault weapon is in the hands of a mass
murderer.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:08.240
We will finally ban these assault weapons
from our streets

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:11.570
that have no other purpose other than to kill.

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:14.729
The federal government banned assault weapons
in 1994,

00:01:14.729 --> 00:01:19.000
when President Clinton signed a bill sponsored
in the Senate by Dianne Feinstein.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.100
Mr. and Mrs America, Turn them all in.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:26.700
The bill expired 10 years later, but Feinstein
is sponsoring a new version of the law.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.180
Meanwhile, seven states and the District of
Columbia

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:31.360
have enacted their own assault weapon bans.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:35.680
There’s little evidence that the 1994 legislation
reduced gun deaths,

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.660
in part because it was mostly a symbolic gesture.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:42.040
Unless you really delve into the specifics
of what these bills do,

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:45.760
you don't understand how utterly arbitrary
they are.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.580
In the original 1994 bill and in the new version
of the legislation

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:53.440
assault weapons are not defined by what they
do but by how they look.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:02:00.880
Feinstein's current bill talks about things
like pistol grips, adjustable stocks, threaded barrels,

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:04.840
in other words, you can have a gun with any
one of those features,

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:06.500
it is now an assault weapon.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:09.980
Exactly the same gun without those features
is not an assault weapon.

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:13.600
And in fact, there are a bunch of examples
like that.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:18.460
Let’s compare the Ruger Mini-14 Ranch Rifle
with the AR-15.

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:22.460
One looks like a hunting rifle and the other
looks like a military weapon.

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:27.360
They fire at the same rate, they fired the
same caliber of ammunition

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.000
and because they have similar barrel lengths,
the ballistics are almost identical.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.780
Now those are single shots.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:39.840
If I wanted to fire this on full semi-automatic,
all I do is keep firing...

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:45.200
Another misconception is that so-called assault
rifles are automatic weapons,

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:50.600
which fire continuously until the trigger
is released or the gun runs out of ammunition.

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:53.690
The federal government banned the manufacture
of new automatic weapons

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:56.240
for civilian use in 1986.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:59.060
Most modern civilian guns are semi-automatic,

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:01.760
which means they only fire one round per trigger
pull.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.080
But if you're talking about, you know,

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:07.860
how many rounds you get out of the gun within
a certain amount of time,

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:11.860
any semiautomatic is gonna fire be capable
of firing the same number of rounds

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:14.940
Originally built for the battlefield,

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:20.100
a defining characteristic of the AR-15 is
the speed and power of the bullet…

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:23.760
Another myth is that assault weapons are more
powerful than other guns.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:29.240
In reality, the power of a firearm depends
mainly on the cartridge, not the gun.

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:33.640
Again, compare the Ruger Mini-14 Ranch Rifle
with the AR-15.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:37.550
Both can shoot the same .223 caliber bullet,
at the same velocity.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:41.840
You will see that lots of hunting rifles are
more powerful,

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:45.140
uh, can do more damage, uh, you know, at the
same distance,

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:47.120
uh, than so called assault weapons.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:50.960
One of the most common cartridges used for
hunting is the .308 Winchester,

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:52.680
which has more than double the impact force

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:55.880
when compared to the ammunition used in an
AR-15.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:57.970
Another common refrain is that assault weapons

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:01.840
can inherently fire more rounds than other
guns before reloading.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:06.140
But it’s the magazine, which is just a detachable
box and a spring,

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:08.380
that determines how many times you can fire.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:11.620
And many guns that are not identified as “assault
weapons”

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:14.000
accept high-capacity magazines.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:20.140
So again, this is not a feature that distinguishes
assault weapons from other kinds of guns.

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:23.540
Banning guns solely based on appearance is
counterproductive

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:28.880
and makes it difficult to have a good-faith
discussion about effective solutions to gun violence.

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:33.860
I’m not going to say everyone has to own
an ar-15, because it's such a great gun.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:35.880
But people have their reasons for wanting
to have them

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:38.270
and the government shouldn't be second-guessing
those reasons

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:41.040
without a very powerful justification.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:45.060
And the justification offered for banning
assault weapons is virtually nonexistent

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:46.700
because it doesn't make sense.

